;; M7 HKT-dispatch reference probe #4 -- Alternative `<|>` / selection shape.
;; NOT a test-suite fixture. Companion to m7-hkt-probe.tur (Functor `fmap`),
;; m7-hkt-probe-bind.tur (Monad `bind`), and m7-hkt-probe-ap.tur (Applicative
;; `ap`).
;;
;; The defining feature of this shape (vs fmap/bind/ap, which all CONSTRUCT
;; their `(f b)` result in-body via `(some ...)`/`(none)`/`(k ...)`) is that the
;; instance body is a SELECTION / PASS-THROUGH:
;;     (alt2 [x y] (if (some? x) x y))
;; the tail returns an EXISTING `(f a)` argument directly. Under the by-value
;; spec the params x/y arrive as the by-value `Option__int`, so returning one of
;; them is already by value -- there is no in-body construct to recover. This is
;; the first shape whose result element is recovered NOT from arg 0 alone but
;; from whichever argument grounds it (arg 0 may be a bare `(none)` whose element
;; is a free tyvar).
;;
;; Run: TUR_M7_HKT=1 ./build/tur run docs/artifacts/m7-hkt-probe-alt.tur; echo $?
;;   -> 42  (TARGET, REACHED 2026-06-19)
;;
;; STATUS (2026-06-19): WORKING end-to-end under TUR_M7_HKT=1 -- exits 42 with
;; NO ascription. With the flag OFF the probe errors by design (no ascriptions).
;;
;; What it took (two coordinated, flag-gated pieces in elab_typeclasses.c):
;;   1. BODY GATE: m7_body_constructs_byvalue now admits an EX_VAR tail of the
;;      applied `(f b)` family (TY_APP) -- a selection/pass-through return of an
;;      existing `(f a)` value -- not just a `#{Construct}` call. The same
;;      m7_body_byvalue_ok flag is computed ONCE up front and now ALSO gates the
;;      by-value result-type commit (layers 1+3), so a carrier-bodied method can
;;      never have its consumer flipped to by-value while its producer still
;;      returns the carrier (the silent-miscompile-to-0 that this shape exposed).
;;   2. ELEMENT RECOVERY: m7_collect_tyvar_bindings now SKIPS a free-tyvar actual
;;      (the element of a bare `(none)`), so a sibling argument that DOES ground
;;      the element wins -- `(alt2 (none) (some 42))` grounds `a` from arg 1,
;;      order-independent. The genuinely-uninferable `(alt2 (none) (none))` stays
;;      un-grounded and falls back to a clean `(type-app ? ?)` error (no
;;      miscompile), mirroring the documented `ap (none) (some _)` caveat.

(defclass MyAlt [^f]
  (alt2 [x : (f a) y : (f a)] : (f a)))

(definstance MyAlt [Option]
  (alt2 [x y]
    (if (some? x) x y)))

(defn main [] : int
  (let [r (alt2 (none) (some 42))]
    (unwrap r)))   ; TARGET: 42
